  "summary": "A strap around the hips, a thousand-year technique: textiles woven with the weaver's own body.",
  "description": "The backstrap loom (telar de cintura) is a portable tension system: one end tied to a post or tree, the other to a leather strap around the weaver's lower back, so that leaning forward and back adjusts warp tension with every shed. Maya women in Guatemala and Chiapas have woven huipils, tzutes, fajas and rebozos on it for two millennia — brocaded with extra-weft motifs of birds, corn, saints and geometric guards that identify village, kin and cosmology. Looms travel; techniques pass mother to daughter; cooperatives (Oxlajuj Ajpop, K'inal Antzetik) now document pattern vocabularies and defend designs from fast-fashion appropriation.",
  "culturalContext": "Backstrap weaving is Maya identity's two-millennium continuum: village-specific brocade vocabularies identify kin and cosmology, cooperatives (Oxlajuj Ajpop) document and defend patterns against appropriation, and the body-tensioned loom remains the mother-daughter technology through which Maya women write their culture in cloth.",
